#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120071 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}u,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}4v\\operatorname{cos}u⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\operatorname{sin}u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v\\operatorname{cos}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-4v\\operatorname{sin}u⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\operatorname{sin}u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v\\operatorname{cos}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{cos}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}u & 0 & -4v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 0 & 2v & 4\\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 2v & 4\\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}u & -4v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 0 & 4\\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\hspace{0.222em}-4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}u,\\hspace{0.222em}2v\\operatorname{cos}u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120072 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = u + v, y = v^{2} + \\sin u, z = uv.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental vector product is $r_u\\times r_v$, in that order. We have $r_u=\\langle1,\\cos u,v\\rangle$ and $r_v=\\langle1,2v,u\\rangle$. Expanding the cross product gives $$r_u\\times r_v=\\langle u\\cos u-2v^2,v-u,2v-\\cos u\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120073 :question/problem "$${∬}_{D}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le \\pi .$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u\\operatorname{cos}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u\\operatorname{cos}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}v & 3\\operatorname{cos}v & 1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}v & -3u\\operatorname{sin}v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}v & 1 \\\\ -3u\\operatorname{sin}v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}v & 1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}v & 3\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}v & -3u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v-6u{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3u\\operatorname{sin}v)^{2}+(2u\\operatorname{cos}v)^{2}+(-6u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+4{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+4{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v)+4{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+4{u}^{2}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v)+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+4{u}^{2}+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+40{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+8)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}}\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{u}^{2}}=u$ since $u\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+8}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120078 :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=3u+2,\\,z=u+2v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $-1\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u+2,\\hspace{0.222em}u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u+2),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u+2),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}-5,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}-5,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{70}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{∬}_{D}\\text{ }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120079 :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨-u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}3v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4\\},\n$$\n\nof radius $2.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{19}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120091 :question/problem "$${∬}_{D}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2v & 2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(-4uv)^{2}+(-4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+32{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}(1+8{u}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{v}^{2}}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120272 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d162245ac19c3ef9ae63d602fb4e188ddfd241a1a604304f6c272c3902ce1e93.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}3t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}2t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{sin}3t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}2t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t+9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t+9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120274 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c76cff47179efaaf490430856b8db1f2b85f0d2000d34725a9c40a9e150ccf6b.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t = 1$ to $t = e$." :question/worked-solution "For $t>0$ the speed simplifies as\n$$\\sqrt{t^{-2}+4+4t^2}=2t+\\frac1t.$$\nThus the length is\n$$\\int_1^e\\left(2t+\\frac1t\\right)dt=[t^2+\\ln t]_1^e=(e^2+1)-1=e^2.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120275 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t+t\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t-t\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩\n$$\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t+t\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t-t\\operatorname{cos}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t+(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t-(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}t\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)+3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120281 :question/problem "Which integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{f}(t)={e}^{t}(\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})$ from  $t=1$ to $t=2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{t}(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}({t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}(1+4+{t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120527 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b614e2c3e6abf0d7ddb8399aa7ea78b7a0e618a965ee7b5481edf08310a45031.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\hspace{0.222em}3{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=1$ to $t=2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}-3)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}+9+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{t}^{2}+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+3t){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(8+6)-(1+3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120528 :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b83db0bb11b8e4bbcc4b04b7bf696879d4d7f4732d6aef7d005f9da2b625526.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨2{t}^{3/2},\\hspace{0.222em}3t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{t})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9t+9} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3(t+1)^{1/2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124194 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z = \\dfrac {\\sqrt {y+1}}{x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y + 1 \\ge  0 \\\\ x + y \\ne  0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y \\ge  - 1 \\\\ y \\ne  - x\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, y \\geq -1, \\: y \\neq -x \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124195 :question/problem "Sketch the domain of $g(x,y)=\\dfrac{1}{\\ln(x+y)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y > 0 \\\\ \\ln   ( x + y ) \\ne  0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y > 0 \\\\ x + y \\ne  1\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y > - x \\\\ y \\ne  1 - x\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\left \\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\, : \\, y \\gt -x, \\, y \\neq 1-x \\right \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we plot the corresponding domain. \n\n- Starting with $y> -x,$ we draw a dashed line $y=-x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\n- Then, we draw a dashed line $y=1-x$ through the shaded region.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6a/6aa1aaeadb177cf5a80bcb12a247725cb3396586e7516c3ba30b46b03a9d2612.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124196 :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = y^2 - \\log_3(x - 7).\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(10,3).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(10,3),$ we substitute $x = 10$ and $y = 3$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,3) & =({y}^{2}-{log}_{3}⁡(x-7)){|}_{(10,3)} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-{log}_{3}⁡(10-7) \\\\  & =9-{log}_{3}⁡(3) \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(10,3)=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124198 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z= \\dfrac{\\ln (2x+y)}{\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive,\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\\begin{cases}2x+y>0 \\\\ x>0\\end{cases}$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{(x, y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, 2x+y >0, \\: x > 0 \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124241 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124242 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124243 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $$\n\\displaystyle f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},\n$$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) &=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$ given that $f(x,y) = (x - y)\\cos y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-(\\operatorname{cos}y+(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute $(x,y)= (1,\\pi)$ into the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,\\pi ) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\pi \\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-(\\operatorname{cos}\\pi +(1-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}\\pi )\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla g(x,y,z)$   at $(2,-1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + xyz.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =2x+yz \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xz \\\\  & =xz \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xy \\\\  & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\text{ }i+(xz)\\text{ }j+(xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,3) & =(2(2)+(-1)(3))\\text{ }i+2(3)\\text{ }j+2(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =1\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124249 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y-4)\\ln (x-2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y-4}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\  \\mathbf i + 0\\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{x-2} &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y-4=0,$ which means $y=4.$  From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x-2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf  0$ at the point $(3,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124251 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y) g(x,y)$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\hspace{1em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\sqrt{xy},\\hspace{1em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla [fg] = g \\, \\nabla\\! f  + f \\, \\nabla\\! g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule shown above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+xy(\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256 :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{x+y} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot {2}^{3}}{(-1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{1} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124257 :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 0 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (3,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(12x-2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4y-4xy)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,3) & =(12(3)-2(3)^{2})i+(4(3)-4(3)(3))j \\\\  & =18\\,i-24\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,-24⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18(x-3)-24(y-3) & =0 \\\\ 18x-24y+18 & =0 \\\\ 3x-4y+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/102f55fe0add794d3ee0f9f044b5e35c4403d4f160fe36e97d737d33ef3abb68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/01/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2 ,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(6x+6y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(6y+6x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ ((6x+6y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(6y+6x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -6y-6x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6x(-x)+3(-x)^{2} & =7+(-x)-3{x}^{2} \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =7-x-3{x}^{2} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7,$ we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261 :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(0,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}g(0,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\ -\\mathrm{∇}f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(1,1)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,\n$$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{y-x}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{1-2}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}t\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124277 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{xz}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124278 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}t,-2\\operatorname{cos}t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,-\\operatorname{sin}t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}t,-2\\operatorname{cos}t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,-\\operatorname{sin}t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t-2\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124287 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nII. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y) = c$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2}{{x}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2 & =c{x}^{2} \\\\ (1-c){x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1,$ then the level curve is $y^2 = 1,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c = 0,$ then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c = 2,$ then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true."}]}
